within Pharmacolibrary.Drugs.C_CardiovascularSystem.C09A_AceInhibitorsPlain.C09AA02_Enalapril;
model Enalapril_Arafat2005_PD_dbp
  "linear exposure-response sweep — Arafat_2005 :: % DBP (percent decrease in systolic blood pressure)"
  extends Pharmacolibrary.Interfaces.PartialExposureResponseSweep(
    E0 = 0,      // [1; % × 0.01]
    Emax = 0, EC50 = 1,      // unused by a line
    exposure_per_s = 1e-06);
  parameter Real slope = -0.22   "[1]";
equation
  response = E0 + slope*exposure;
  annotation(experiment(StartTime = 0, StopTime = 1000, Interval = 2),
    Documentation(info = "<html><body><h4>Enalapril_Arafat2005_PD_dbp</h4><p>The steady-state exposure-response curve of Arafat T et al., <i>Journal of clinical pharmacy and therapeutics 30(4):319–328</i> 2005 (<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2710.2005.00646.x\">doi:10.1111/j.1365-2710.2005.00646.x</a>) — percent decrease in systolic blood pressure [%] against enalaprilat ng/mL — obtained by sweeping the driver instead of simulating a dose. Model family: linear.</p><h4>The parameters, in the paper's terms</h4><pre>  E0    =          0 %        drug-free response (baseline) (% DBP predose, Q324)
  slope =      -0.22          change in response per unit of exposure (S, Q335)</pre><p>The algebra is <code>response = E0 + slope*exposure</code>; this model only supplies the numbers. The x axis is one ng/mL of enalaprilat per second of simulated time (exposure is in kg/m3): there is no state, no dose and no kinetics — plot <code>response</code> against <code>exposure</code> (or <code>time</code>).</p><h4>What it reproduces</h4><pre>  exposure = 0         response 0   (drug-free)</pre></body></html>", revisions = "<html><body><ul><li>11/2026 generated by pharmacolibrary-dev export.pharmacolibrary_models from Arafat_2005: parameters extracted from the paper by the pharmacolibrary-dev pipeline, model built by its engineer, reviewed: curated candidate</li></ul></body></html>"));
end Enalapril_Arafat2005_PD_dbp;
